{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-parity-auditor","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan depends on exact bash-vs-Python behavioral parity across finalize, cleanup, rebase, force-push, and teardown contracts.","prompt_body":"Compare the proposed Python changes against the bash reference surfaces named in the plan, especially scripts/implement-finalize.sh, scripts/local-cleanup.sh, and related helper scripts. Look for missing branches, wrong status-token mapping, accidental behavior changes to bash, or places where the plan says parity but targets a different remote, baseline, retry policy, or failure severity. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"run-log-recovery","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Manifest recovery and fail-closed report/write ordering spans run_logs, finalize, and ship and is easy to specify inconsistently.","prompt_body":"Inspect whether the plan fully preserves the run-log manifest recovery contract across python/run_logs.py, python/finalize.py, and python/ship.py. Hunt for gaps in partial-status tagging, recovery_ok propagation, report-skip ordering, teardown commit behavior, and cases where status=done could still be written after recovery failure. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"state-token-plumbing","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The proposed change introduces auxiliary result fields and pending-state lifecycle that must remain consistent across modules and tests.","prompt_body":"Trace the planned status fields, auxiliary KVs, ship-state fields, and CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING lifecycle through finalize, ship, ci_monitor, and tests. Look for schema mismatches, stale state overwrites, unclear serialization or hydration steps, and status names that conflict with the stated bash STATUS-only rule. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
